{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And now, let it be magnified, etc.</b> There are several points to raise: (a) His saying <b>וְעַתָּה</b> (\"and now\"). (b) His saying <b>יִגְדַּל</b> (\"let it be magnified\") implies that He will do some thing through which He will be magnified further, yet we find no act that He performed in this matter to bring about magnification. (c) His saying <b>נָא</b> — if the intention is \"now,\" then he already said עַתָּה יִגְדַּל; and if it is an expression of request, why did he not put it first? (d) His saying <b>כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבַּרְתָּ</b> (\"as You spoke\") — what did HaShem speak, and where did He speak it? (e) The word <b>לֵאמֹר</b> (\"saying\") — to whom would they say these things?" "<b>However,</b> on the grounds that Moses out of wisdom offered an argument that is persuasive — namely, that there would be a great desecration of the Name in killing them — he added to speak that now there is an additional advantage to the magnification of His greatness, may He be blessed, if He turns back from His wrath and Israel enters the Land, which was not the case previously regarding this matter. That is: after the spies told of the wondrous might of the Land and the strength of its warriors, and despite this He dispossessed them before Israel — through this the power of HaShem will be magnified." "And His saying <b>כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבַּרְתָּ</b> — this refers to the utterance ״שְׁלַח לְךָ וְגוֹ׳ אֲשֶׁר אֲנִי נוֹתֵן לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Numbers 13:2), which we explained in its place: the intent of this utterance is that it is detached from the aspect of comprehension according to the aspect of capability, but rather as a gift that the Creator gives to the children of Israel, as we wrote there. And this particular point HaShem said to him, to say to Israel — and that is the meaning of ״דִּבַּרְתָּ לֵאמֹר״ (\"You spoke, saying\")." "And His saying <b>נָא</b> — means \"now,\" and he doubled to say \"now\" twice, as will be explained according to their words (Genesis Rabbah 52) that they said: ״וְעַתָּה״ means nothing other than repentance. Because Moses said before HaShem things that are not fitting to be said about the Master — even though he was quoting the words of the nations and not his own words, heaven forbid — nevertheless it is unseemly. And you will find a parallel in the matter of one who hears the blessing of HaShem from the mouths of witnesses: even though they are only testifying to what the wicked man who blessed said, nevertheless the one who hears must tear his garments (Sanhedrin 60a). And likewise, when Moses uttered the words of the nations against the honor of HaShem, may He be blessed, he confessed and repented for having put words of contempt from his mouth against the Creator, blessed be He — and that is the meaning of ״וְעַתָּה״." "The verse also alluded, in the utterance <b>וְעַתָּה</b>, that He should accept the repentance of Israel. And His saying <b>יִגְדַּל נָא</b> is along the lines of what they said, may their memory be for a blessing (Berakhot 34b): in the place where penitents stand, even the perfectly righteous cannot stand. And the reason that His name, may He be blessed, becomes more sanctified when the wicked improve their ways — that is His saying <b>יִגְדַּל נָא</b>, meaning: the greatness of HaShem will be greater now when they return in repentance after the rebellion. He also alluded in the word נָא along the lines of what they said (Avot 4:17): ״One hour of repentance and good deeds in this world...״" "<b>Further</b>, the verse may be expounded in this manner: <b>and now, let grow great</b> — meaning, what I ask of HaShem is not to settle the pardon upon all the blasphemers, but rather ״וְעַתָּה יִגְדַּל נָא כֹּחַ ה׳״ — meaning, to take vengeance upon the wicked blasphemers, who are the spies, for when HaShem executes judgment upon the wicked, His name becomes great and exalted. And His saying <b>as You spoke</b> refers to what He said (Numbers 14:12): ״אַכֶּנּוּ בַדֶּבֶר״, which we explained in its place according to one of the approaches as referring back to the spies. And He was precise in saying ״נָא״ — meaning, now, at the time of the transgression, and not grant them delay; and so it was, as it is written (Numbers 14:37): ״וַיָּמֻתוּ הָאֲנָשִׁים מוֹצִיאֵי דִבַּת הָאָרֶץ רָעָה בַּמַּגֵּפָה לִפְנֵי ה׳״, and see what I explained there. And His saying <b>to say</b> — perhaps Moses asked HaShem that He declare the matter to them before they die, so that they may see and be dismayed. Or it may intend <b>to say</b> to Israel: See these who spoke falsely — what came of them? — to enslave them so they would not return to provoke again; and so David said (Psalms 9:17) ״בְּפֹעַל כַּפָּיו נוֹקֵשׁ רָשָׁע הִגָּיוֹן סֶלָה״. And he alluded in the initial letters of <b>וְעַתָּה יִגְדַּל נָא כֹּחַ</b> to the name Elohim, and to the name ״אֲדֹנָי״, which the verse records as well, also indicating judgment; and afterward he returned to awaken mercy upon the entirety of the people, as it is written ״סְלַח נָא לַעֲוֹן הָעָם הַזֶּה״." "<b>Further</b>, Moses intended in the statement <b>and now</b> etc., in response to what HaShem said to him ״וְאֶעֱשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל וְעָצוּם״ — that even though HaShem would relent from the evil He had spoken, for this reason HaShem would not retract His good word. And you will find that they said, their memory for a blessing (Makkot 11a): a sage's curse, even if conditional, etc., and all the more so a blessing, and all the more so the words of the living Elohim. And this is His saying <b>and now</b> — meaning, and now too let the power of HaShem grow great, which He spoke to say ״וְאֶעֶשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״ — to establish a great nation that exists now, and to increase His doing by adding a great nation from Moses. And you will find that so it was, as it is written ״וּבְנֵי רְחַבְיָה רָבוּ לְמַעְלָה״ (1 Chronicles 23:17), and they said, their memory for a blessing (Berakhot 7a): more than sixty myriad. And He was precise in saying ״דִּבַּרְתָּ לֵאמֹר״, because at the time He spoke harshly against Israel He exchanged Moses for a good measure; therefore he said ״דִּבַּרְתָּ״ — a harsh utterance against Israel, ״לֵאמֹר״ — a soft saying to Moses." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend <b>as You spoke</b> — that he would become a great nation, and His saying <b>to say</b> means corresponding to what He said ״וְעָצוּם מִמֶּנּוּ״ — that their quality would be stronger than theirs:" "<b>And</b> we have cause to investigate this: why did Moses not request this thing when He said so to him at the making of the calf, for there too it was said to him (Exodus 32:10) ״וְאֶעֱשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״? And it is possible that here he came with an argument because the matter had issued from the mouth of the Most High twice, and that is what he was precise about in his statement <b>as You spoke to say</b> — meaning, You spoke at the incident of the calf and You said now at the incident of the spies." "<b>Further</b>, it appears that in the first statement HaShem said only ״וְאֶעֱשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״ and did not say ״וְעָצוּם מִמֶּנּוּ״, and according to one of the approaches I explained regarding the statement ״וְעָצוּם מִמֶּנּוּ״ the intention is that the great nation that would come from Moses is from the people itself, as I wrote; and therefore, after he prayed that the crown remain in its place among the people who came out of Egypt, he was concerned for his own good that HaShem had told him ״וְאֶעֱשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ״ etc. — that it would be annulled, for HaShem did not say He would make him a great nation except from the people; therefore he said <b>״יִגְדַּל נָא כֹּחַ ה׳״</b> etc. — whereas in the incident of the calf, HaShem said only ״וְאֶעֱשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״; by inference, even though HaShem relented and was appeased regarding the evil He had spoken against His people, for this reason the good would not be annulled even if it had been conditional, and HaShem blessed be He would implant new souls to be fruitful and multiply. And perhaps this was the intention of their words, their memory for a blessing, who said (Berakhot 32a) that Moses said before the Holy One blessed be He: A throne of three legs cannot stand on one leg etc.; Israel who have three pillars etc., until here. And it is difficult: was Moses not one of them, that he would say of his children that they are of one leg? But according to our words it is justified, because the nation that would arise from Moses is implanted souls who did not come from Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob; and accordingly they have but one leg." "<b>Further,</b> a reason appears for why he prayed here: because it was promised concerning the nation that would emerge from Him that it would be exalted in the degree of holiness and purity, such that it would not blaspheme HaShem; therefore it was that he prayed, ״יִגְדַּל כֹּחַ ה׳״ — concerning this specific matter. And he was precise in saying ״יִגְדַּל נָא״ — meaning, we find (Berakhot 33a) that the choice to do good is not in the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He; <b>ועתה יגדל נא כח ה׳</b> — even with respect to this matter, to bring about a soul inclined toward good deeds."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {3 "The reference קריעה (tearing garments) upon hearing a blasphemy is based on Sanhedrin 60a, though the text cites סנהדרין ס. (= 60 in gematria-style dotted abbreviation).", 5 "The initial letters of וְעַתָּה יִגְדַּל נָא כֹּחַ (ו,י,נ,כ) are understood as alluding to divine names through remez/letter-play; the logic of how they map to Elohim and Adonai is not spelled out in the Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:57.241437881Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}